# Line endings are content here, so git is told rather than asked.
#
# Without a rule, core.autocrlf decides, and it is a per-machine setting.
# In the project this was carried from, a Windows checkout produced CRLF
# and a Linux one LF, and the two disagreed about the contents of every
# multi-line Turtle literal -- because a CRLF inside a literal is part of
# that literal's value, not formatting around it. That was 77 triples
# across 8 files, and 11 of them were SPARQL text a validator executes
# rather than prose somebody reads.
#
# This repository has the same exposure. `skos:definition` and
# `rdfs:comment` carry multi-line text throughout the APQC modules, and
# every commit made here so far has warned "LF will be replaced by CRLF".
#
# The rule is repository-wide rather than limited to *.ttl, for a reason
# that only shows up later: any digest recorded over a file git is free to
# re-encode is not a digest. Pin the corpus alone and the first artifact
# recording a checksum of a JSON report will match on the machine that
# wrote it and nowhere else.
#
# Applied before the corpus grew, which is the cheap moment. Renormalising
# rewrites every text blob, so doing it later means doing it across more
# files and comparing digests taken on both sides of the change.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Redundant under the rule above, and kept anyway: the corpus is the
# reason the rule exists, and a future narrowing of `*` should not
# silently take the corpus with it.
*.ttl text eol=lf
*.tsv text eol=lf

# Declared binary so content detection is not what stands between these
# and a corrupting newline translation.
*.pdf -text
*.zip -text
*.png -text
.DS_Store -text
